I found Hito Steyerl's Poor Images (2011) piece really revealing in understanding the complexity around 'the image," in the context of the world today. With rapid advances in technology and social media, what was once perceived as being valuable or important about an image has changed. After I persevered through some of the complex language in the essay, I found it really exciting how she makes us reconsider and question what the real significance of an image is now. Is the quality of the image and honoring how it was originally intended to be viewed important? Or is it worth sacrificing these things to disseminate it among more people, with the possibility of having a greater impact?
Within the essay, she does not present 'the poor image," as a perfect alternative to commercial media or capitalism. She highlights the contradiction that the poor image has the ability to resist commercial media by being easily shared. This trait counters capitalism's need for profit. Yet, it also feeds into capitalism because its fast-moving form fits our shortened attention spans and makes it easy to consume. This reminded me of the constant frustration of feeling like this system is completely inescapable, even when we try to resist it. I like that Steyerl looks at the poor image from both sides, recognizing that it can be a form of resistance while also being absorbed into the system it is resisting.
Reading her essay made me think of a work I saw this year in Venice by artist Yuyan Wang called Boring Billion. The single channel video was composed purely from found footage online. What was once a tutorial for repairing a machine or a recording of robot testing, becomes part of a larger message about technological structures vulnerability. She presents a world where machines "mirror our living systems," which made me feel uncomfortable but also sympathetic. The original purpose of each video or image in the work is no longer important. Rather our attention is drawn to the assembled footage as an unfolding story into this seemingly mechanical landscape of madness. The work connects to Steyerl's idea of circulation. Wang is sharing videos that reach an audience who may have never encountered the original. In turn, giving the images a new life and meaning beyond their initial purpose.